# §R — THE RECONCILIATIONS (where the slices disagreed, and which I took)

| # | the disagreement | taken | why |
|---|---|---|---|
| **R-1** | **When a face and a sibling surface disagree, who is wrong?** Floor 1 as drafted makes the lawful face the defendant and the stale card the record | **A finding carries a third field: WHICH SIDE IS THE RECORD.** Where the sibling is engine PROSE or a stale snapshot, the face stands and a WIRING row is filed | In four of five measured collisions (C1, C2, C3, C5) the corpus face is the truthful party. A floor that charges it would force the rewrite to re-introduce ruin-blindness to agree with a defect. **Vetoable; this is the ruling stress-3 asked for** |
| **R-2** | **Is "frozen vs live" a contradiction?** C11: DS-DEF-1 bands a frozen `readiness.score` while SubstrateTab renders the live band; the desk measured a village at frozen CRITICAL / live `strained` | **NO.** A first-survey band and a live band are two measurements with two vocabularies and are not contradictory. A refuter may not fail a face for disagreeing with a band on the other clock | The product already carries the tie-break language ("as judged at the first survey"; the banner's eyebrow reads "Internal Security · First Survey"). Without this ruling every DS-DEF-1, DS-DEF-2-economic and DS-DEF-3 face is refutable on every town and the re-cut's whole gain is spent on false FAILs |
| **R-3** | **Does a `Barracks` license "the garrison"?** ET-2 D-7 kept it (`institutionLadders.js:23`, Barracks→Garrison is an UPGRADE); W12b and RT2.1-06 struck it | **STRUCK.** "The garrison" is lawful wherever `inst.hasGarrison` or the garrison bucket resolves, Barracks included | `priorityHelpers.js:46` matches `'barracks'`; `defenseInstitutionBuckets.js:89` puts it IN the bucket; the row's own desc is "housing for guards or **small garrison**"; the panel prints "The garrison patrols" off the flag. The ladder is an upgrade path, not a claim about what the page prints |
| **R-4** | **"the court".** W14-court kept the criminal half; RT2.1-14 / OV-8 struck the word entirely | **BOTH: the WORD is free, the criminal PROCEDURE is the finding** (F1-12) | `hasCourtSystem` is the engine's own reading of a court system; a trial, a gaoling or a gallows off a hall-only town contradicts `safetyProfile.js:333` |
| **R-5** | **"the guard".** ADDENDUM 13 X-D2 licensed it only where `hasGarrison` resolves AND no `Military/Guard` faction is named; W13c grounded it on `hasMilitaryInst`; the RT slice on "any law body" | **The contradiction is against `safetyProfile.js:300`/`:309`, which fire when NO law body resolves.** X-D2's faction-name half is STRUCK — it never denied anything | The denial is a printed sentence; the faction-name condition was a licence rule |
| **R-6** | **Is `hasWatch` "town and up" a law?** | **NO** (RT2.5R-04). The test is the FLAG on THIS town; a custom roster can seat a watch at any tier | This also protects custom content (F1-30) |
| **R-7** | **What fixes a wall's material?** W11a: the row's printed description. A-7/L-51: the fortification chain's stone class. W11-19 NF-5: the two contradict each other on a palisade | **THE ROW'S OWN PRINTED DESCRIPTION** (`institutionVocabulary.js`) fixes the MATERIAL (F1-32); **the chain** grounds the SOURCE bar only (F1-33) | The description is what the reader sees beside the prose; the chain never prints a material word. So a palisade is timber (the description) and its source is still unsayable (the chain) |
| **R-8** | **The muster ROLL.** W13e struck it; A-4 and F1-03 keep it as a citation | **A roll may be MENTIONED freely; a roll CITED as a record needs the holder** (`Citizen militia` / `Muster training`). Never a number on it (F2-01) | W24's record-word bar is struck; F1-24's holder rule is not |
| **R-9** | ⛔ **R-viii′'s no-headcount half.** ET-2 D-31 keeps "never a thinning muster, never desertion"; W1-W10 and W16d strike it | **STRUCK.** Men drifting off slowly is licensed; an EMPTIED town is not (F4-04) | `defenseGenerator.js:186-187`'s own comment reads "unpaid soldiers desert slowly" and the multiplier actually REDUCES the funded force, so slow thinning is model-TRUE. ADDENDUM 12 barred it on a CELL-OVERLAP ground (R-DST-A, DS-DEF-5's cell) — a composition rule, not a truth. **This is a live amendment to a ratified ruling; vetoable** |
| **R-10** | **Floor 2 has no verdict token** (ET-2 finding 17; RT-2-5 gap 1) | **MINTED: `[F2]` / PROMISE**, with §F2 written as nine grammar-decidable rows | Roughly forty rows' only survivor is floor 2; with tokens for floors 1, 3 and 4 alone a refuter reads it as struck |
| **R-11** | **Visibility.** RT §4.2 kept covert-field rows as CONTRADICTION and noted the floors name no AUDIENCE floor; W26a called it MODEL | **MODEL (F4-13).** The covert flags ARE the engine's positive model of audience | Rows resting on an AUTHORED mark with no field behind them (VIS-12 foreign funding, VIS-18) remain **STRUCK and unrefutable** — recorded as residual risk in CHAIR-NOTE §4 |
| **R-12** | **Does floor 1 reach across tabs?** | **YES — the unit is the DOSSIER, not the tab** | The PDF prints the tabs as one document (`src/pdf/`), and "the WHOLE town's record" is the honest reading. Consequence: DS-DEF-4 ↔ DS-POW-6 and the pay-gate twins get a shared ruling before either desk is written |
| **R-13** | **Floor 4's wording: "walls never decay"** | **RE-CUT** (F4-01): *no material decay clock*, plus *no face asserts the permanence of any institution row* | As written, floor 4 tells the writer permanence IS the model, and `calamityKernel.js:259-275` demotes and ruins walls. The old wording does not merely fail to bar the error — it invites it |

